1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,260
Welcome to chapter twelve of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:11,372
This is the chapter where our hand built MiniGPT finally learns something.

3
00:00:11,372 --> 00:00:21,182
We cover the next token objective, cross entropy, data loaders, the five step training loop, validation, perplexity, checkpoints and sampling.

4
00:00:21,182 --> 00:00:28,920
I ran the notebook on a CPU for the full three epochs, and the numbers I got are very different from the book's.

5
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:36,243
I'll show you why: a padding bug hiding in one argument, and a data pipeline that wastes a lot of compute.

6
00:00:36,243 --> 00:00:40,457
Then I'll show a fixed version and how much better it trains.

7
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:48,624
So far we have a tokenizer, embeddings, attention and a complete MiniGPT, but every weight is random.

8
00:00:48,624 --> 00:00:51,015
Today we train it on Shakespeare.

9
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:52,899
Next chapter we fine tune.

10
00:00:52,899 --> 00:00:56,304
Everything follows the chapter twelve notebook.

11
00:00:57,154 --> 00:00:58,287
Here's the plan.

12
00:00:58,287 --> 00:01:00,270
Training as a feedback loop.

13
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:01,261
The objective.

14
00:01:01,261 --> 00:01:03,740
Cross entropy and the ignore index.

15
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:05,014
The data pipeline.

16
00:01:05,014 --> 00:01:08,272
The five step recipe with warmup and clipping.

17
00:01:08,272 --> 00:01:11,033
Validation, overfitting and perplexity.

18
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:12,945
Checkpoints and generation.

19
00:01:12,945 --> 00:01:17,265
Then my run against the book, the bug, and a better pipeline.

20
00:01:17,265 --> 00:01:19,106
Then exercises and review.

21
00:01:19,956 --> 00:01:20,732
Some terms.

22
00:01:20,732 --> 00:01:26,023
Cross entropy is minus the log of the probability given to the right token.

23
00:01:26,023 --> 00:01:28,493
The learning rate is the step size.

24
00:01:28,493 --> 00:01:30,327
Warmup ramps it up gently.

25
00:01:30,327 --> 00:01:32,796
An epoch is one pass over the data.

26
00:01:32,796 --> 00:01:34,771
Perplexity is e to the loss.

27
00:01:34,771 --> 00:01:39,428
Overfitting is when training loss falls but validation loss rises.

28
00:01:39,428 --> 00:01:43,167
And a checkpoint saves everything you need to resume.

29
00:01:44,017 --> 00:01:44,620
Part one.

30
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,363
What training actually is.

31
00:01:47,213 --> 00:01:51,706
The book uses a nice engineering framing, which I've redrawn here.

32
00:01:51,706 --> 00:01:54,020
The target is the true next token.

33
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:55,994
The error signal is the loss.

34
00:01:55,994 --> 00:01:58,648
The controller is the optimizer, AdamW.

35
00:01:58,648 --> 00:02:07,088
Its action is a weight update, and the plant output is the model's new predictions, which we compare with the targets again.

36
00:02:07,088 --> 00:02:10,083
Measure, adjust, repeat, thousands of times.

37
00:02:10,933 --> 00:02:11,535
Part two.

38
00:02:11,535 --> 00:02:14,410
What exactly are we asking the model to do?

39
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:17,485
Take one sequence of six tokens.

40
00:02:17,485 --> 00:02:22,838
Drop the last token to get the inputs, and drop the first to get the targets.

41
00:02:22,838 --> 00:02:26,523
Now every position has a job: predict the next token.

42
00:02:26,523 --> 00:02:30,625
Six tokens give five training examples in one forward pass.

43
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:40,775
And because of the causal mask from chapter ten, position two can only see positions zero to two, so asking it for token three is a fair question.

44
00:02:41,625 --> 00:02:43,300
Here's the book's helper.

45
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:52,077
Slice the inputs, slice and clone the targets, then replace padding with minus one hundred, which cross entropy ignores by default.

46
00:02:52,077 --> 00:02:54,958
Notice that the default padding id is zero.

47
00:02:54,958 --> 00:02:59,246
Hold on to that, because it comes back to bite us in part eight.

48
00:03:00,096 --> 00:03:00,794
Part three.

49
00:03:00,794 --> 00:03:02,443
How we score a prediction.

50
00:03:03,293 --> 00:03:10,006
Cross entropy looks only at the probability the model gave the correct token, and takes minus its log.

51
00:03:10,006 --> 00:03:13,100
Ninety percent gives a loss of about point one.

52
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:15,338
Ten percent gives two point three.

53
00:03:15,338 --> 00:03:17,509
One percent gives four point six.

54
00:03:17,509 --> 00:03:29,554
A brand new model that spreads probability evenly over fifty thousand tokens scores about ten point eight, which is why a loss stuck near ten or eleven means nothing has been learned.

55
00:03:30,404 --> 00:03:38,929
PyTorch's cross entropy wants a two D table of scores and a one D list of correct classes, so we flatten batch and time together.

56
00:03:38,929 --> 00:03:47,322
The targets use reshape rather than view, because slicing off the first column leaves a tensor that isn't contiguous in memory.

57
00:03:48,172 --> 00:03:53,548
Before any training, here's what my random model wrote after "The fair maiden".

58
00:03:53,548 --> 00:03:54,296
Pure noise.

59
00:03:54,296 --> 00:04:00,013
Every token is about equally likely, so whichever one happens to score highest wins.

60
00:04:00,013 --> 00:04:01,782
That's our starting point.

61
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:03,289
Part four.

62
00:04:03,289 --> 00:04:05,128
Getting data into the model.

63
00:04:05,978 --> 00:04:10,864
The data is TinyShakespeare, about one point one million characters.

64
00:04:10,864 --> 00:04:16,970
With the GPT-2 tokenizer that's about three hundred and thirty eight thousand tokens.

65
00:04:16,970 --> 00:04:21,209
The dataset class cuts the raw text into fixed size chunks.

66
00:04:21,209 --> 00:04:25,520
Note that the chunks are measured in characters, not tokens.

67
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:26,885
That matters later.

68
00:04:27,735 --> 00:04:32,443
The collate function turns a list of strings into a padded tensor.

69
00:04:32,443 --> 00:04:40,291
It tokenises them, truncates at one hundred and twenty eight tokens and pads each batch to its longest member.

70
00:04:40,291 --> 00:04:45,570
GPT-2 has no padding token, so the notebook borrows the end of text token.

71
00:04:45,570 --> 00:04:49,922
Its id is fifty thousand two hundred and fifty six, not zero.

72
00:04:50,772 --> 00:04:54,272
A batch is the group processed together for one update.

73
00:04:54,272 --> 00:04:56,627
An epoch is one pass over everything.

74
00:04:56,627 --> 00:05:03,055
The book's arithmetic example says ten thousand examples give about three hundred and twelve batches.

75
00:05:03,055 --> 00:05:15,530
But the actual notebook, with two hundred and fifty six character chunks, has three thousand nine hundred and twenty one training chunks, so one epoch is only one hundred and twenty three batches.

76
00:05:15,530 --> 00:05:22,467
And the book's printed training output also shows three hundred and twelve, which doesn't match its own code.

77
00:05:23,317 --> 00:05:34,251
A seeded ninety ten split, a partial to bind the tokenizer into the collate function, and data loaders that shuffle the training set but not the validation set.

78
00:05:34,251 --> 00:05:35,754
Standard and sensible.

79
00:05:36,604 --> 00:05:37,262
Part five.

80
00:05:37,262 --> 00:05:38,908
The heart of the chapter.

81
00:05:39,758 --> 00:05:43,510
Every neural network trains with the same five steps.

82
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:47,261
Zero the gradients, because PyTorch accumulates them.

83
00:05:47,261 --> 00:05:48,747
Run the forward pass.

84
00:05:48,747 --> 00:05:49,951
Compute the loss.

85
00:05:49,951 --> 00:05:50,942
Backpropagate.

86
00:05:50,942 --> 00:05:53,065
Optionally clip the gradients.

87
00:05:53,065 --> 00:05:57,949
Then let the optimizer step, and advance the learning rate scheduler.

88
00:05:57,949 --> 00:05:59,577
Repeat for every batch.

89
00:06:00,427 --> 00:06:02,714
The learning rate is the step size.

90
00:06:02,714 --> 00:06:06,503
Too big and you overshoot, too small and it takes forever.

91
00:06:06,503 --> 00:06:11,860
Warmup starts tiny because early gradients from random weights aren't trustworthy.

92
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:18,002
The notebook uses AdamW at three e minus four, with a hundred warmup steps and a linear decay.

93
00:06:18,002 --> 00:06:25,515
One thing I noticed: with only a hundred and twenty three steps per epoch, warmup takes up most of the first epoch.

94
00:06:26,365 --> 00:06:28,922
Gradient clipping is a safety limiter.

95
00:06:28,922 --> 00:06:37,335
If the overall gradient norm is bigger than one, the whole gradient is scaled down, so one bad batch can't wreck the weights.

96
00:06:37,335 --> 00:06:43,325
It has to happen after backward, when the gradients exist, and before the optimizer step.

97
00:06:44,175 --> 00:06:46,972
Here's the training function, condensed.

98
00:06:46,972 --> 00:06:51,516
It's the five step recipe exactly, after calling model dot train.

99
00:06:51,516 --> 00:06:57,669
But look at the loss: ignore index zero, with a comment saying padding is token id zero.

100
00:06:57,669 --> 00:07:02,983
We just saw that the padding id is fifty thousand two hundred and fifty six.

101
00:07:02,983 --> 00:07:04,591
I'll come back to this.

102
00:07:05,441 --> 00:07:06,130
Part six.

103
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:08,428
Is it learning, or memorising?

104
00:07:09,278 --> 00:07:12,806
Always watch training and validation loss together.

105
00:07:12,806 --> 00:07:14,813
If both fall, you're healthy.

106
00:07:14,813 --> 00:07:21,802
If training keeps falling but validation turns upward, the model is memorising, which is overfitting.

107
00:07:21,802 --> 00:07:27,752
If both stay high, the model is underfitting: too small, too little training or a bug.

108
00:07:28,602 --> 00:07:36,741
Perplexity is e to the validation loss, and you can read it as how many equally likely tokens the model is torn between.

109
00:07:36,741 --> 00:07:40,743
Ten point eight means guessing across the whole vocabulary.

110
00:07:40,743 --> 00:07:45,016
Six means it has learned word frequencies, around four hundred.

111
00:07:45,016 --> 00:07:46,779
Four and a half is ninety.

112
00:07:46,779 --> 00:07:52,816
And it's only fair to compare perplexities that use the same tokenizer and the same data.

113
00:07:53,666 --> 00:07:57,468
Evaluation runs without gradients and with dropout off.

114
00:07:57,468 --> 00:08:04,104
It sums the loss and divides by the number of counted tokens to get average loss and perplexity.

115
00:08:04,104 --> 00:08:09,980
Early stopping halts training when validation loss has risen several epochs in a row.

116
00:08:09,980 --> 00:08:15,511
With only three epochs, the book's patience of three can never actually trigger.

117
00:08:16,361 --> 00:08:17,148
Part seven.

118
00:08:17,148 --> 00:08:18,580
Saving and sampling.

119
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:22,096
A checkpoint needs more than the weights.

120
00:08:22,096 --> 00:08:29,511
AdamW keeps two running averages for every parameter, and the scheduler knows where it is in the warmup and decay.

121
00:08:29,511 --> 00:08:34,064
Save all of it, and keep the checkpoint with the best validation loss.

122
00:08:34,914 --> 00:08:39,387
Generation now samples instead of always picking the top token.

123
00:08:39,387 --> 00:08:45,067
Divide the last position's logits by a temperature, softmax, and draw one token.

124
00:08:45,067 --> 00:08:47,622
Below one sharpens the distribution.

125
00:08:47,622 --> 00:08:49,184
Above one flattens it.

126
00:08:49,184 --> 00:08:53,231
As temperature goes to zero you get greedy decoding back.

127
00:08:53,231 --> 00:08:56,781
Chapter fourteen goes deeper with top k and top p.

128
00:08:57,631 --> 00:08:58,271
Part eight.

129
00:08:58,271 --> 00:09:00,426
What actually happened when I ran it.

130
00:09:01,276 --> 00:09:06,628
Here's the book's printed run next to mine, same notebook, same seed, on a CPU.

131
00:09:06,628 --> 00:09:10,694
The parameter count is sixteen million, not ten point eight.

132
00:09:10,694 --> 00:09:15,979
One epoch is a hundred and twenty three batches, not three hundred and twelve.

133
00:09:15,979 --> 00:09:25,194
My validation loss went six point two two, five point six six, five point five one, ending at a perplexity of two hundred and forty six.

134
00:09:25,194 --> 00:09:28,785
The book prints three point seven five and forty two.

135
00:09:28,785 --> 00:09:41,184
That's a big gap, and the perplexity table from part six tells us what it means: my model has learned word frequencies and some line structure, nothing like a perplexity of forty two.

136
00:09:42,034 --> 00:09:46,898
And here's what it actually wrote after three epochs, for "to be or not to be".

137
00:09:46,898 --> 00:09:52,686
It has learned the shape of a play: short lines, speaker tags in capitals, thee, thy and hath.

138
00:09:52,686 --> 00:09:54,348
But these aren't sentences.

139
00:09:54,348 --> 00:09:59,889
The book shows the king is a man of great virtue and honour, which my run is nowhere near.

140
00:09:59,889 --> 00:10:04,445
The notebook's own closing message says training makes all the difference.

141
00:10:04,445 --> 00:10:08,509
It does, but three epochs on this setup isn't enough to get there.

142
00:10:09,359 --> 00:10:10,864
Here's the first bug.

143
00:10:10,864 --> 00:10:21,541
Ignore index zero doesn't ignore padding, because in GPT-2 token zero is the exclamation mark, a real character, about point six percent of the text.

144
00:10:21,541 --> 00:10:28,635
Meanwhile the real padding token, fifty thousand two hundred and fifty six, is counted in the loss.

145
00:10:28,635 --> 00:10:35,155
Batches are padded to about a hundred tokens when the average chunk is about seventy eight.

146
00:10:35,155 --> 00:10:41,891
I counted: twenty two percent of all target positions are padding that the model is scored on.

147
00:10:41,891 --> 00:10:49,630
The fix is to mask using the attention mask, or the real pad id, and set those targets to minus one hundred.

148
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:53,271
The second issue is design rather than a bug.

149
00:10:53,271 --> 00:11:01,394
Chunks are measured in characters, so they're only about seventy eight tokens long, less than the model's hundred and twenty eight.

150
00:11:01,394 --> 00:11:03,006
Every batch needs padding.

151
00:11:03,006 --> 00:11:09,206
And three epochs is only three hundred and sixty nine weight updates, a hundred of which are warmup.

152
00:11:09,206 --> 00:11:17,701
A better pipeline tokenises the whole text once and samples random hundred and twenty eight token windows, which needs no padding at all.

153
00:11:18,552 --> 00:11:31,126
Here's my fix: the same sixteen million parameter model, same learning rate and warmup, but trained on random hundred and twenty eight token windows from the token stream, with no padding at all.

154
00:11:31,126 --> 00:11:34,350
The two curves overlap early, which is reassuring.

155
00:11:34,350 --> 00:11:47,118
At step three fifty, mine was at five point three seven, against the notebook's five point five one after three sixty nine steps, and my metric counts every token, with no padding to make it easier.

156
00:11:47,118 --> 00:11:57,436
Running on to twelve hundred steps reached a validation loss of four point eight seven, a perplexity of about a hundred and thirty, roughly half the notebook's.

157
00:11:57,436 --> 00:12:00,015
Still nowhere near the book's forty two.

158
00:12:00,015 --> 00:12:03,175
It took about an hour and a half on a shared CPU.

159
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:08,188
And here's what that model writes when prompted with King Henry.

160
00:12:08,188 --> 00:12:17,620
It answers with Queen Margaret, a real character from the history plays, uses line breaks, thou and my lord, and sounds roughly like Shakespeare.

161
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:19,051
But it makes no sense.

162
00:12:19,051 --> 00:12:26,532
That's honestly what a perplexity of a hundred and thirty looks like on a small model trained for an hour on a CPU.

163
00:12:27,382 --> 00:12:30,440
Why do I doubt the book's perplexity of forty two?

164
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:38,271
Three hundred and thirty eight thousand tokens, sixteen million parameters, and only three hundred and sixty nine small updates.

165
00:12:38,271 --> 00:12:49,465
Neither the notebook nor my better pipeline gets anywhere near that, and the printed sample about a man of great virtue and honour reads like an illustration rather than a logged run.

166
00:12:49,465 --> 00:12:55,094
The lesson: trust your own logs, and always check what your ignore index actually points at.

167
00:12:55,944 --> 00:12:56,741
Part nine.

168
00:12:56,741 --> 00:12:57,458
Practice.

169
00:12:58,308 --> 00:13:00,440
Quick notes on the exercises.

170
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:07,422
A custom dataset smaller than about a megabyte overfits quickly, so watch the validation curve.

171
00:13:07,422 --> 00:13:16,609
In the learning rate sweep, one e minus five barely moves, three e minus four is healthy, and one e minus two tends to spike.

172
00:13:16,609 --> 00:13:20,137
Low temperature loops, high temperature is soup.

173
00:13:20,137 --> 00:13:24,033
For resuming, reload the optimizer and scheduler too.

174
00:13:24,033 --> 00:13:27,781
Early stopping needs more epochs than its patience.

175
00:13:27,781 --> 00:13:32,265
And the perplexity improvement is one minus final over first.

176
00:13:33,115 --> 00:13:33,991
Short answers.

177
00:13:33,991 --> 00:13:35,868
The five steps, as we've seen.

178
00:13:35,868 --> 00:13:39,998
Without shifting, the model can see the answer and learns to copy.

179
00:13:39,998 --> 00:13:44,253
Ignore index skips positions, but only if it points at the right id.

180
00:13:44,253 --> 00:13:57,267
Ten thousand over thirty two is three hundred and twelve point five, so three hundred and thirteen batches including the last partial one, or one thousand five hundred and sixty five updates over five epochs.

181
00:13:57,267 --> 00:14:01,147
And saving only weights resets the optimizer and the schedule.

182
00:14:01,997 --> 00:14:08,393
Your practice: run the notebook, change the ignore index to the real pad id, and compare.

183
00:14:08,393 --> 00:14:11,771
Log loss every few steps, not just every epoch.

184
00:14:11,771 --> 00:14:16,226
And try the stream sampler from my notes for a thousand steps.

185
00:14:17,076 --> 00:14:21,724
This video is based on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

186
00:14:21,724 --> 00:14:32,443
The explanations and diagrams are my own, but the book goes deeper, so please support the author and buy it at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

187
00:14:32,443 --> 00:14:37,615
The code is on GitHub at First LLM slash code, under the MIT licence.

188
00:14:37,615 --> 00:14:41,588
Thanks for watching, and see you in chapter thirteen.

